  "text": "To solve this problem, we need a function that removes adjacent duplicates from a list of integers, leaving only one instance of each consecutive duplicate element. \n\n### Strategy:\n\nWe can iterate through the list and build a new list. For each element, we check if it is the same as the last element in the new list. If it is not, we add it to the new list. This way, only non-consecutive duplicates will remain.\n\nWe'll implement this in Python and test it with the provided `unittest` test cases.\n\n### Solution Code:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    new_list = [lst[0]]  # Initialize with the first element\n    for item in lst[1:]:\n        if item != new_list[-1]:\n            new_list.append(item)\n    return new_list\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- The `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function:\n  - Initializes a new list with the first element of the input list.\n  - Iterates through the rest of the elements and appends each to the new list only if it is different from the last element in the new list.\n- This ensures only non-consecutive duplicates are removed.\n- The test cases verify that the function works correctly for various scenarios.",
